{
  "assessment": "The source provides a complete theoretical model for heat transport in a chain of M Coulomb islands with N chiral ballistic channels in the Heat Coulomb Blockade regime. It derives the temperature profile, heat flow, and Lorenz ratio, with explicit formulas for the large-chain limit. The key quantitative cluster is the normalized Lorenz ratio L/L0 as a function of chain length M, with public M in {20,100} and private N=1 versus N=2. For N=1, the heat flow is exactly kappa0*(T_S^2-T_D^2)/(M+1) and L/L0=1. For N=2, in the large chain limit M>>N, L/L0 ≈ sqrt(M+1)/2. The extraction is case_authoring_ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Patrice Roche, Breakdown of the Wiedemann-Franz Law in an Interacting Quantum Hall Metamaterial, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/twbt-z82z",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/twbt-z82z",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2510.10391v3"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper theoretically analyzes heat transport through a 1D chain of M Coulomb islands connected by N chiral ballistic edge channels in the integer quantum Hall regime, focusing on the Heat Coulomb Blockade (HCB) regime where charge dynamics are frozen. It derives the temperature profile, heat flow, and Lorenz ratio, showing a violation of the Wiedemann-Franz law with the Lorenz ratio scaling as sqrt(M) for N>1. The main quantitative results are the heat diffusion equation, the approximate temperature profile, the heat flow formula, and the Lorenz ratio formula in the large-chain limit.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "heat_diffusion_equation",
      "statement": "The temperature profile of the chain is governed by a discrete diffusion equation balancing local diffusion by neutral modes (LN/RN) and a non-local equalizing term from the split neutral (SN) mode. The equation is (N-1)[T_{k+1}^2 - 2T_k^2 + T_{k-1}^2] = 4(T_k^2 - T_mid^2)/(M+1), with T_mid^2 = (T_S^2 + T_D^2)/2. The decay length is Delta = sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/2.",
      "equations": [
        "(N-1)[T_{k+1}^2 - 2T_k^2 + T_{k-1}^2] = 4(T_k^2 - T_mid^2)/(M+1)",
        "Delta = sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/2"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["N", "M", "T_k", "T_mid", "Delta", "T_S", "T_D"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "T_mid_sq",
          "value": 0.000725,
          "uncertainty": "Not reported; computed from T_S=35 mK and T_D=15 mK as (T_S^2+T_D^2)/2.",
          "unit": "K^2",
          "role": "Mean squared temperature defined as (T_S^2+T_D^2)/2",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 100,
              "line_end": 102,
              "support": "T_mid^2=(T_S^2+T_D^2)/2 is defined in the diffusion equation context."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 81,
              "line_end": 81,
              "support": "Figure caption states T_S=35 mK, T_D=15 mK for the temperature profile."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 97,
          "line_end": 102,
          "support": "The temperature profile is obtained from power balance, leading to the diffusion equation (N-1)[T_{k+1}^2-2T_k^2+T_{k-1}^2]=4(T_k^2-T_mid^2)/(M+1) with T_mid^2=(T_S^2+T_D^2)/2, and decay length Delta=sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/2."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "lorenz_ratio_scaling",
      "statement": "In the large chain limit M >> Delta (equivalently M >> N), the Lorenz ratio is approximately L = sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/N * L0, where L0 is the standard Lorenz number. This scaling holds for N>1 and M >> N.",
      "equations": [
        "L = sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/N * L0"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["L", "N", "M", "L0"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "L0",
          "value": 2.44e-08,
          "uncertainty": "Not reported; standard value.",
          "unit": "V^2/K^2",
          "role": "Standard Lorenz number L0 = pi^2 k_B^2 / (3 e^2)",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 44,
              "line_end": 44,
              "support": "L0 = pi^2 k_B^2 / (3 e^2) ≈ 2.44e-8 V^2/K^2 is stated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "one",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "Exact constant.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "The integer 1 appearing in the formula.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 142,
              "line_end": 142,
              "support": "The formula contains (N-1) and (M+1)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "half",
          "value": 0.5,
          "uncertainty": "Exact constant.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "The exponent 1/2 in the square root.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 142,
              "line_end": 142,
              "support": "The formula contains a square root, i.e., exponent 1/2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "two",
          "value": 2.0,
          "uncertainty": "Exact constant.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "The integer 2 appearing in the denominator for N=2.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 142,
              "line_end": 142,
              "support": "The formula contains N in the denominator; for N=2 this is 2."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 139,
          "line_end": 146,
          "support": "The Lorenz ratio is defined as L = [Qdot/(T_S-T_D)] / [G_el (T_S+T_D)/2] and in the large chain limit M >> Delta it approximates to sqrt((N-1)(M+1))/N * L0, scaling as sqrt(M)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "singular_N1_heat_flow",
      "statement": "In the singular case N=1, the heat flow retains its non-local contribution only due to the SN mode. Since all sites have the same temperature T_mid, the dependence on the sites' temperature vanishes, and we have Qdot = kappa0*(T_S^2-T_D^2)/(M+1). The normalized Lorenz ratio L/L0 equals 1.",
      "equations": [
        "Qdot = kappa0*(T_S^2-T_D^2)/(M+1)",
        "L/L0 = 1"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["Qdot", "kappa0", "T_S", "T_D", "M", "L", "L0"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "kappa0",
          "value": null,
          "uncertainty": "Not reported; defined symbolically as kappa0 = pi^2 k_B^2 / (6h).",
          "unit": "W/K^2",
          "role": "Thermal conductance quantum per channel, defined symbolically as kappa0 = pi^2 k_B^2 / (6h)",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 90,
              "line_end": 90,
              "support": "kappa0 = pi^2 k_B^2 / (6h) is defined."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "unity",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "Exact constant.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "The normalized Lorenz ratio for N=1.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 146,
              "line_end": 146,
              "support": "For N=1, the ratio remains equal to 1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 133,
          "line_end": 133,
          "support": "In the singular case N=1 the heat flow retains its non-local contribution only due to the SN mode. Since all sites have the same temperature T_mid, the dependence on the sites' temperature vanishes, and we have Qdot=kappa0(T_S^2-T_D^2)/(M+1)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 146,
          "line_end": 146,
          "support": "For N=1, the ratio remains equal to 1, since the heat flow matches the diffusive solution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "hc_regime",
      "statement": "The system is in the Heat Coulomb Blockade (HCB) regime where k_B T << hbar G_0 / C, so charge on each island is frozen and current noise cannot flow through the capacitance.",
